WEBVTT

00:08.967 --> 00:14.889
If Whyman really did throw in
several Medusas with outright hostility,

00:15.473 --> 00:18.059
truly determined to wipe out
humanity for good...

00:29.112 --> 00:31.238
There's one petrification device here!

00:31.239 --> 00:33.657
Are you for real?

00:33.658 --> 00:36.244
Awesome! We're really lucky!

00:36.995 --> 00:38.038
"Lucky"?

00:38.872 --> 00:42.751
This doesn’t seem like just that...

00:49.466 --> 00:53.303
This is a sickening, yet beautiful...

00:54.137 --> 00:55.263
menace.

00:56.639 --> 00:58.141
ORIGINAL WORK: RIICHIRO INAGAKI, BOICHI
SHUEISHA "JUMP COMICS"

02:43.204 --> 02:45.373
One meter, three seconds.

03:03.141 --> 03:04.100
Forgive me,

03:04.601 --> 03:06.685
but If I turn to stone,

03:06.686 --> 03:09.522
please pour
the precious Revival Fluid on me.

03:13.860 --> 03:17.113
One meter, one second!

03:19.782 --> 03:23.076
One meter, one second!
One meter, one second!

03:23.077 --> 03:25.996
One meter, one second!

03:25.997 --> 03:27.497
That looks fun!

03:27.498 --> 03:29.082
Let's help him out!

03:29.083 --> 03:31.377
One meter, one second!

03:31.961 --> 03:32.836
Huh...

03:32.837 --> 03:35.422
Just as we expected,

03:35.423 --> 03:38.635
all of these petrification devices'
batteries are dead.

03:40.053 --> 03:41.346
Of course.

03:41.846 --> 03:43.389
Even if it's been used for years,

03:44.098 --> 03:47.226
a device with just enough power
to cover all of Treasure Island

03:47.227 --> 03:50.605
couldn’t possibly still work
after covering the entire Earth.

03:51.606 --> 03:54.776
Yes... This is a magnificent view.

03:56.402 --> 03:59.363
<i>All of these new scientific weapons</i>

03:59.364 --> 04:02.367
<i>lit up one after another</i>

04:03.034 --> 04:04.952
<i>thousands of years ago,</i>

04:04.953 --> 04:08.915
<i>turning the entire Earth
into the Stone World.</i>

04:10.250 --> 04:11.960
Or so it goes.

04:12.460 --> 04:15.630
An overwhelming amount of hostility,

04:16.464 --> 04:19.299
all to turn humanity into stone.

04:19.300 --> 04:20.926
Yes.

04:20.927 --> 04:25.473
There was no way to escape
from the very beginning.

04:28.142 --> 04:30.019
Xeno, dear...

04:30.603 --> 04:32.437
All right, your right hand.

04:32.438 --> 04:36.191
You just pocketed one
from around there, didn’t you?

04:36.192 --> 04:37.943
A Medusa...

04:37.944 --> 04:40.946
So you said something all deep

04:40.947 --> 04:44.199
to shift everyone’s attention.

04:44.200 --> 04:47.035
You really going to try
that move on a mentalist?

04:47.036 --> 04:49.413
I don't know what you're talking about...

04:49.414 --> 04:51.248
You're done for! Don’t mess with us!

04:51.249 --> 04:53.458
Well, who cares?

04:53.459 --> 04:55.253
The batteries are dead, anyway...

04:55.837 --> 04:58.171
He can't do anything
as long as we keep an eye on him.

04:58.172 --> 05:01.591
Besides, it might be a good idea
to let him take an interest

05:01.592 --> 05:04.512
and investigate, so we can use his advice.

05:05.013 --> 05:08.015
These Medusas might be our only chance

05:08.016 --> 05:11.436
to beat Stanley's elite army.

05:13.980 --> 05:19.276
But there's no point in having so many
if we can't use them at all.

05:19.277 --> 05:21.321
That's not exactly true.

05:21.821 --> 05:24.991
Having this many
does give us some options.

05:25.742 --> 05:27.327
Right, Senku?

05:28.703 --> 05:29.662
Yes.

05:37.211 --> 05:38.545
I see!

05:38.546 --> 05:42.341
Even if only Joel and the other experts
can take it apart cleanly,

05:43.217 --> 05:45.260
just breaking it is easy!

05:45.261 --> 05:47.930
We already knew that
from Matsukaze’s testimony.

05:48.890 --> 05:51.058
When the Medusa
was one of our most critical assets,

05:51.059 --> 05:54.227
a risky disassembly
was out of the question.

05:54.228 --> 05:57.773
But now, we can take it apart
as much as we want and analyze it.

05:57.774 --> 05:58.858
Right?

06:00.526 --> 06:02.736
Science kids love this stuff.

06:02.737 --> 06:06.824
Let’s take our time
and enjoy a good dismantling session!

06:11.746 --> 06:16.166
Just where are Stanley and the others
headed in this ship,

06:16.167 --> 06:17.668
Matsukaze?

06:18.252 --> 06:21.172
I’m truly sorry.
I don’t know myself, either.

06:21.756 --> 06:25.259
Senku and the others
should have escaped safely by now.

06:25.968 --> 06:28.345
Don't worry.

06:28.346 --> 06:30.848
I'm sure the Science Club kids

06:31.349 --> 06:33.725
are just relaxing and taking it easy.

06:33.726 --> 06:37.729
Who knows? They won't snap easily either.

06:37.730 --> 06:39.732
Do not worry.

06:40.233 --> 06:42.025
<i>Based on the motorcycle tracks,</i>

06:42.026 --> 06:46.279
<i>their escape route is almost certainly
down the Amazon River.</i>

06:46.280 --> 06:49.784
The kids ought to build a new ship.

06:50.409 --> 06:52.911
<i>Considering that huge time loss,</i>

06:52.912 --> 06:56.124
<i>no matter how big the detour,
we will definitely catch up.</i>

06:56.707 --> 07:00.377
If the enemy’s got a new ship,
won’t it be hard to spot?

07:00.378 --> 07:02.505
Its appearance doesn't matter.

07:03.131 --> 07:06.134
The radar never fails to detect intruders.

07:06.717 --> 07:09.637
There’s absolutely nothing that can hide

07:10.304 --> 07:13.683
from the 'eye of science', our radar.

07:23.526 --> 07:26.945
I’m sure you’d love
to keep investigating here forever,

07:26.946 --> 07:28.948
but we'll leave once the new ship's ready.

07:29.532 --> 07:33.161
Yes, I'm just stacking
as many Medusas as I can.

07:34.328 --> 07:35.538
That's true.

07:36.122 --> 07:38.457
No matter how you look at it,

07:38.458 --> 07:41.001
we can’t go to sea on a raft.

07:41.002 --> 07:43.171
But...

07:43.963 --> 07:46.381
Isn't this ship's shape too weird?

07:46.382 --> 07:48.633
I think so too.

07:48.634 --> 07:49.676
Is it?

07:49.677 --> 07:52.013
It’s kind of jagged,
but it looks pretty cool.

07:52.597 --> 07:55.182
You guys like jagged stuff...

07:55.183 --> 07:58.269
I think it'd be cuter
if it were more roundish.

07:58.853 --> 08:00.812
Everything’s on a slant,

08:00.813 --> 08:02.607
tilted as upward as possible.

08:04.358 --> 08:05.609
This shape...

08:05.610 --> 08:06.818
Yes.

08:06.819 --> 08:09.946
As a former sonar expert,
you probably already know this.

08:09.947 --> 08:12.324
When Stanley and his crew come after us,

08:12.325 --> 08:14.952
the only way to beat their radar net

08:16.412 --> 08:18.456
<i>is to build a stealth ship!</i>

08:19.040 --> 08:20.832
What's a stealth ship?

08:20.833 --> 08:22.751
Stealth means being covert.

08:22.752 --> 08:26.880
In other words,
it's 'invisible' to radar detection.

08:26.881 --> 08:28.673
Um, well...

08:28.674 --> 08:31.259
Is this how big the science project is?

08:31.260 --> 08:34.472
It’s starting to feel like
real modern warfare science, right?

08:37.683 --> 08:40.602
Ryusui looks really excited.
This is funsies.

08:40.603 --> 08:42.145
Of course!

08:42.146 --> 08:43.939
Even for a want-it-all guy like me,

08:43.940 --> 08:45.899
this is my first time with a stealth ship!

08:45.900 --> 08:49.862
That's because civilians don't need
to hide from radars...

08:50.446 --> 08:53.740
So, jagged ships
can't be detected by radar?

08:53.741 --> 08:56.576
Yes, that's somehow right.

08:56.577 --> 08:58.078
We've talked about this before.

08:58.079 --> 09:00.748
Since radar watches for reflected signals,

09:01.332 --> 09:02.582
<i>a normal ship would...</i>

09:02.583 --> 09:03.792
NORMAL SHIP

09:03.793 --> 09:06.670
<i>Whoa, it'd be in full view!</i>

09:06.671 --> 09:08.672
<i>But if it's all jagged and slanted...</i>

09:08.673 --> 09:09.589
ALL JAGGED AND ON A SLANT

09:09.590 --> 09:11.467
<i>It's all invisible!</i>

09:12.051 --> 09:13.927
As radio wave-absorbing materials,

09:13.928 --> 09:17.265
we’ll use rubber
loaded with carbon powder.

09:18.891 --> 09:22.103
Rubber can really be used
for almost anything!

09:22.937 --> 09:27.190
There are plenty of designs
to hide from radio waves.

09:27.191 --> 09:30.277
Some are military secrets,
so I don’t know the details either.

09:30.278 --> 09:32.697
We’ve got no choice
but to go through trial and error.

09:34.574 --> 09:38.703
But our NASA guy over here
might know a lot about it.

09:39.287 --> 09:42.080
I’m more than willing
to offer scientific help

09:42.081 --> 09:44.583
when it concerns my own safety.

09:44.584 --> 09:48.545
But I have no reason
to help deceive Stanley.

09:48.546 --> 09:50.840
You're so mean, Xeno!

10:03.144 --> 10:05.812
STEALTH SHIP ACQUIRED!!

10:05.813 --> 10:09.274
This jagged one also somehow looks cool!

10:09.275 --> 10:10.443
Right?

10:11.027 --> 10:13.278
Its performance is what matters, idiot.

10:13.279 --> 10:15.323
We're testing its stealth performance.

10:26.917 --> 10:30.212
Does it show on the radar?
Or is it invisible?

10:31.130 --> 10:32.047
It's...

10:32.048 --> 10:33.716
How is it?

10:34.383 --> 10:38.554
Um, well... kind of stealthy?

10:39.138 --> 10:42.349
More like a bit hard to spot
for something this size, somehow...

10:42.350 --> 10:43.892
"Kind of"?

10:43.893 --> 10:46.103
You mean, we can't really hide it?

10:46.854 --> 10:49.773
There's no way we can create
a military secret from scratch.

10:49.774 --> 10:52.068
Now it’s all about
giving it everything we’ve got.

10:53.944 --> 10:57.072
A bit hard to spot
for something this size?

10:57.073 --> 10:58.990
So, if it were huge and heavy,

10:58.991 --> 11:01.242
it would be easy to spot with a radar?

11:01.243 --> 11:03.788
Of course.
It’s no different from the naked eye.

11:04.372 --> 11:05.665
In that case,

11:06.248 --> 11:09.377
<i>why did such a ridiculously huge
amount of chunks fall that day?</i>

11:10.461 --> 11:12.379
<i>People in the 21st century</i>

11:12.380 --> 11:14.590
<i>didn’t even notice
until the very last moment.</i>

11:17.093 --> 11:22.098
Not every asteroid approaching Earth
can be detected.

11:22.598 --> 11:24.892
But certainly, this amount...

11:25.393 --> 11:29.230
I didn't hear anything
from any observatory, not even NASA.

11:30.481 --> 11:32.483
This strange shape...

11:39.990 --> 11:40.825
It's not showing...

11:41.742 --> 11:42.910
in the radar!

11:45.204 --> 11:47.831
To avoid being discovered by humans?

11:47.832 --> 11:49.749
That's vindictive.

11:49.750 --> 11:52.168
It's sheer hostility.

11:52.169 --> 11:54.879
To avoid radars...

11:54.880 --> 11:58.050
Yes, radio waves
are being absorbed somewhere.

11:58.551 --> 12:01.178
It must be some kind of
mysterious circuit with a catch.

12:01.929 --> 12:03.096
Then...

12:03.097 --> 12:06.142
Why don't we just stick
a bunch of these on the ship?

12:09.520 --> 12:11.354
Our radar-dodging,

12:11.355 --> 12:14.566
science-powered stealth ship is done!

12:14.567 --> 12:17.236
FUTURISTIC STEALTH SHIP ACQUIRED!

12:18.904 --> 12:21.322
This is the first craft of the New World

12:21.323 --> 12:23.325
that surpasses 21st-century technology!

12:25.244 --> 12:26.744
Here we are now,

12:26.745 --> 12:30.082
stepping into the science of the future!

12:33.210 --> 12:34.837
Science is...

12:38.466 --> 12:40.634
It's actually elegant...

12:50.519 --> 12:54.773
We actually managed to use
these Medusas as cover for our ship.

12:55.941 --> 12:59.861
Senku, the epitome of rationality,
must be really glad about this.

12:59.862 --> 13:03.239
Yes, science is all about efficiency.

13:03.240 --> 13:05.701
We’ve got to use whatever we can.

13:06.368 --> 13:08.661
Even if we don't understand
how it works at all!

13:08.662 --> 13:09.996
Yes.

13:09.997 --> 13:12.917
We’ll be studying how it works
from here on.

13:14.877 --> 13:18.464
We must be grateful to have
such a ridiculous number of samples.

13:19.715 --> 13:24.303
Yes, if we could analyze and reactivate
this huge number of Medusas...

13:27.848 --> 13:30.309
<i>we could stand a chance against Stanley.</i>

13:32.269 --> 13:33.811
The chance of victory

13:33.812 --> 13:36.690
would literally be in our hands, right?

13:37.816 --> 13:39.443
About that...

13:40.277 --> 13:44.155
We're analyzing a dangerous weapon
meant to stop Stanley.

13:44.156 --> 13:46.032
Is it really okay

13:46.033 --> 13:48.576
for the great Dr. Xeno
to be involved in this?

13:48.577 --> 13:52.540
I think it’s even more dangerous
to neglect basic research

13:53.040 --> 13:57.044
and fall behind
just for political reasons.

13:57.711 --> 14:00.046
Of course, Senku,

14:00.047 --> 14:05.552
I’m not sharing with you
any discoveries you missed.

14:05.553 --> 14:09.264
Do as you want.
I’ll share as much as I can

14:09.265 --> 14:11.308
to speed things up.

14:18.148 --> 14:19.607
There's a black diamond

14:19.608 --> 14:22.068
inside of every Medusa.

14:22.069 --> 14:24.113
Just as Joel's reports said...

14:24.822 --> 14:28.284
Although the parts are incredibly tiny
after destruction,

14:28.784 --> 14:30.493
knowing about them is a big deal.

14:30.494 --> 14:32.161
We might not have noticed them

14:32.162 --> 14:35.373
<i>without the elegant disassembly report
from Joel,</i>

14:35.374 --> 14:37.626
<i>the master craftsman
of fine jewelry watches.</i>

14:38.127 --> 14:41.338
<i>Truly, knowledge is power.</i>

14:41.839 --> 14:43.923
Did the diamond turn dark

14:43.924 --> 14:47.969
because the crystal's structural changes
distorted the light's refractive index?

14:47.970 --> 14:49.971
Are there patterns to how it darkens?

14:49.972 --> 14:52.349
Not even one.

14:55.853 --> 14:57.437
We’ve analyzed about a thousand of them,

14:57.438 --> 15:00.190
but every diamond
is ridiculously different.

15:00.774 --> 15:03.736
So, it's not necessarily
a designed pattern.

15:04.403 --> 15:06.529
Their changes are completely chaotic.

15:06.530 --> 15:08.490
In other words, it's simple damage.

15:12.912 --> 15:13.871
Wait, this is awesome!

15:14.788 --> 15:17.333
There's one diamond
that hasn't darkened at all!

15:17.917 --> 15:18.959
Could this be...

15:19.960 --> 15:22.253
If the diamonds are like their batteries,

15:22.254 --> 15:24.464
doesn’t that mean this one is new?

15:24.465 --> 15:26.841
Good. Let’s look for one.

15:26.842 --> 15:30.054
A Medusa with a clean diamond in it.

15:37.978 --> 15:39.855
One meter, three seconds.

15:45.694 --> 15:48.072
So maybe it’s not actually new?

15:48.572 --> 15:51.408
It's more like an unexploded bomb...

15:53.452 --> 15:54.827
There's a crack.

15:54.828 --> 15:58.873
Most likely, the atomic bonds
along the cleavage plane are weak,

15:58.874 --> 16:01.377
causing it to break
along those easily fractured surfaces.

16:01.961 --> 16:03.336
You're right.

16:03.337 --> 16:06.507
There are cracks on every unused diamond.

16:08.050 --> 16:10.803
Could you explain it to me?

16:11.303 --> 16:13.680
I don't mind, Senku.

16:13.681 --> 16:16.100
It's not like you wouldn't notice too.

16:18.477 --> 16:20.019
The other unused diamonds

16:20.020 --> 16:23.231
are cracked too, and unusually small.

16:23.232 --> 16:27.443
So, these are the new ones left over
after some error caused the failure.

16:27.444 --> 16:31.991
On the other hand, all the used,
blackened diamonds are scratch-free.

16:32.616 --> 16:35.743
They've all got the same shape too.

16:35.744 --> 16:37.036
So that means...

16:37.037 --> 16:39.832
Yes, it's just a hypothesis.

16:40.416 --> 16:43.126
<i>If we put in a brand-new,
scratch-free diamond</i>

16:43.127 --> 16:45.838
<i>that’s ten billion percent a perfect fit,</i>

16:47.464 --> 16:51.760
the Medusa could recover and reboot.

16:52.886 --> 16:53.928
Which means...!

16:53.929 --> 16:56.055
We're making diamonds!

16:56.056 --> 16:57.473
GOAL DIAMOND

16:57.474 --> 16:59.059
PETRIFICATION DEVICE

16:59.685 --> 17:01.395
- What?
- What?

17:01.979 --> 17:04.313
You're acting crazy, as always...

17:04.314 --> 17:06.899
There's no way they can make diamonds!

17:06.900 --> 17:09.986
And if they could,
wouldn’t they become insanely rich?

17:09.987 --> 17:11.280
At least in the Old World.

17:11.947 --> 17:14.824
<i>But it's possible that Senku can do it.</i>

17:14.825 --> 17:16.159
<i>Teehee!</i>

17:16.160 --> 17:20.330
<i>I should’ve become friends with Senku
in the Old World!</i>

17:21.081 --> 17:24.752
<i>The expert engineer Joel
will find a way to make it work.</i>

17:25.377 --> 17:27.712
You have to make the diamonds.

17:27.713 --> 17:29.589
Even if you say so,

17:29.590 --> 17:32.759
we have no scientists here now.

17:32.760 --> 17:35.803
You and Dr. Xeno are away, Senku.

17:35.804 --> 17:37.138
<i>Wrong.</i>

17:37.139 --> 17:39.725
<i>This is not a job for an expert scientist.</i>

17:40.476 --> 17:42.102
<i>Anyone can do it.</i>

17:42.728 --> 17:45.105
<i>No matter if you’re a saint or a murderer,</i>

17:45.898 --> 17:49.610
<i>whoever makes it,
the result will always be the same.</i>

17:51.445 --> 17:53.030
That's science for you.

17:55.199 --> 17:57.659
<i>The materials you'll need
to make diamonds are...</i>

17:58.160 --> 18:00.788
<i>- Alcohol. That's it.</i>
- Really?

18:01.622 --> 18:04.123
<i>You can make it
out of any alcohol you find around.</i>

18:04.124 --> 18:05.793
<i>It'll take time, though.</i>

18:08.837 --> 18:09.796
Found some!

18:09.797 --> 18:13.050
There's alcohol in Dr. Xeno's room!

18:13.634 --> 18:16.094
<i>You need to heat it up a lot. That's it.</i>

18:16.095 --> 18:17.721
Isn't that a bit too sloppy?

18:18.305 --> 18:20.098
To achieve extreme heat,

18:20.099 --> 18:22.183
we're going to make a tungsten filament.

18:22.184 --> 18:23.810
Tung...?

18:23.811 --> 18:25.770
It sounds familiar...

18:25.771 --> 18:28.564
Isn't it the stone I dug up?

18:28.565 --> 18:31.150
Yes, it’s the strongest
heat-resistant metal

18:31.151 --> 18:33.361
ever used in light bulbs.

18:33.362 --> 18:35.446
If we wrap a metal wire around it,

18:35.447 --> 18:38.784
<i>we'll have a heat-resistant filament.</i>

18:43.122 --> 18:45.456
We'll put the alcohol in boiling water

18:45.457 --> 18:47.501
and let it steam.

18:48.085 --> 18:51.004
And we're going to heat it up
using that filament, right?

18:52.589 --> 18:55.049
We just have to connect some electricity
and heat it up.

18:55.050 --> 18:56.510
Isn’t it easy?

19:00.889 --> 19:02.141
What was that explosion?

19:05.352 --> 19:06.894
I don't... know...

19:06.895 --> 19:12.483
Why don't we leave this
to that enemy mechanic, Brody?

19:12.484 --> 19:14.152
No way.

19:14.153 --> 19:18.157
There's more to this diamond-making
thing Senku asked for.

19:18.657 --> 19:21.493
Don't leave your production line
in enemy hands.

19:22.536 --> 19:24.162
Senku said that

19:24.163 --> 19:27.040
science is not just for geniuses.

19:28.000 --> 19:29.168
There's no one else.

19:29.751 --> 19:31.170
We'll have to make the diamonds

19:32.004 --> 19:33.547
ourselves...!

19:35.465 --> 19:38.844
Could it be the air mixed
and caused the explosion?

19:48.896 --> 19:51.731
- It worked!
- Not yet.

19:51.732 --> 19:55.736
Only after we've completely
expelled the alcohol vapor.

19:57.279 --> 19:58.237
Good.

19:58.238 --> 20:01.325
This way, there’s definitely
no air left inside the bottle.

20:01.909 --> 20:03.410
I'll try again then.

20:14.796 --> 20:17.007
- It's starting to come out.
- Really?

20:17.674 --> 20:19.008
It's average charcoal.

20:19.009 --> 20:21.385
Maybe the temperature is too low?

20:21.386 --> 20:22.846
I'll increase the current.

20:33.023 --> 20:34.440
Like this...?

20:34.441 --> 20:35.776
Here I go.

20:39.905 --> 20:42.281
Now there's no current at all.

20:42.282 --> 20:44.617
Just why?

20:44.618 --> 20:47.662
- I just don't get it!
- Could it be broken...?

20:47.663 --> 20:50.415
These two connection points
are stuck together.

20:52.084 --> 20:54.085
You might think it’s silly,

20:54.086 --> 20:56.170
but having the electrical connectors
stuck together

